  "core_diagnostic_findings": {
    "why_epoch_1_selected": "Epoch 1 achieved the global minimum validation loss for all four conditions. Across all conditions, training loss steadily and monotonically decreased from epoch 1 to 11 (e.g. Cond A: 0.673 -> 0.301; Cond D: 1.015 -> 0.528), confirming that the optimizer was actively updating model parameters and gradient descent was functioning correctly. However, on validation, cross-entropy loss on minority failure classes (c1 and c2) immediately increased after epoch 1 (e.g. Cond A: c1 rose from 2.50 to 3.09; Cond D: c2 rose from 7.22 to 7.96), followed by generalization degradation on class 0 as well. Because validation loss never improved below the epoch 1 level, the early stopping patience counter reached 10 at epoch 11, halting execution and correctly preserving the epoch 1 checkpoint.",
    "early_stopping_behavior": "Early stopping functioned exactly as designed. It monitored validation loss with a patience of 10. All four conditions ran for exactly 11 epochs (epoch 1 best + 10 patience epochs) and stopped early.",
    "checkpoint_epoch_alignment": "Verified that all four saved checkpoints (transformer_condA_unweighted.pt, transformer_condB_moderate.pt, transformer_condC_stronger.pt, transformer_condD_higher.pt) record 'epoch': 1 and contain the exact weights and val_loss from epoch 1. There was no checkpoint mismatch or failure to restore the best model.",
    "optimizer_and_scheduler_audit": "Optimizer: AdamW was configured with lr=5e-4 and weight_decay=1e-4. Gradients were non-zero across all layers (first-batch grad norms between 1e-4 and 0.45). Scheduler: ReduceLROnPlateau correctly monitored val_loss. Because val_loss did not improve for 5 epochs, learning rate was halved from 5e-4 to 2.5e-4 at epoch 7 across conditions.",
    "parameter_change_audit": "Model parameters changed substantially during training. L2 distance from initial weights grew from epoch 1 to epoch 11 across all layers (e.g. input_proj.weight L2 shift grew from ~0.15 at epoch 1 to ~0.50 at epoch 11). Checkpoints did NOT suffer from zero gradients, frozen weights, or stale states.",
    "loss_weighting_and_reduction_audit": "The weight tensor [w0, w1, w2] was correctly passed to nn.CrossEntropyLoss with correct class ordering: index 0 = Normal, index 1 = Warning, index 2 = Critical. The loss reduction was 'mean'. In PyTorch, nn.CrossEntropyLoss(weight=w, reduction='mean') computes sum(w_yi * loss_i) / sum(w_yi). Thus, the effective loss is normalized by the total batch weight rather than the batch size, keeping the overall scale of gradients bounded.",
    "condition_independence_audit": "All four conditions used fresh model instances initialized from seed 42. All four saved checkpoints have distinct SHA-256 hashes and distinct parameter tensors, confirming independent runs without weight reuse or stale checkpoints."
  },
